Client Device Setup: 5 Android + 5 iPhone + 1 Mac

This is written for beginners. Follow steps in order—don’t worry if your screen looks slightly different.

A) Restarting devices

Android – normal restart
	1.	Hold the Power button until the power menu appears.
	2.	Tap Restart (or Power off, then press Power again).

Android – force restart (frozen)
	1.	Hold Power + Volume Up (or Power + Volume Down) for ~10–15 sec.
	2.	Release when the logo appears.

iPhone – normal restart (Face ID models)
	1.	Hold Side + either Volume until the slider appears.
	2.	Slide Power off → after screen is off, hold Side to turn on.

iPhone – force restart (unresponsive)
	•	iPhone 8 or later: Vol Up → Vol Down → hold Side till Apple logo.
	•	iPhone 7/7 Plus: hold Side/Top + Volume Down.
	•	iPhone 6s or earlier: hold Home + Side/Top.

B) Android: turn on Developer options + USB debugging (do once per phone)
	1.	Open Settings > About phone.
	2.	Tap Build number 7 times (enter PIN if asked). You’ll see “You are now a developer.”
	3.	Go to Settings > System > Developer options (location varies by brand).
	4.	Toggle Developer options = On.
	5.	Toggle USB debugging = On → OK.
	6.	Connect to the Mac; when prompted Allow USB debugging? choose Always allow → OK.

If “Build number” isn’t in About phone: check Settings > About phone > Software information.

C) iPhone: turn on Developer Mode (iOS 16+; do once per phone)
	1.	Connect iPhone to the Mac (cable).
	2.	On iPhone, when asked Trust This Computer? tap Trust and enter passcode.
	3.	On the Mac, open Xcode > Window > Devices and Simulators and Pair if shown.
	4.	On iPhone, go to Settings > Privacy & Security > Developer Mode.
	5.	Toggle Developer Mode = On. iPhone will restart → after restart tap Turn On.

If Developer Mode switch is missing
	•	Ensure iOS 16 or later.
	•	Keep phone connected and Xcode open.
	•	Install/run a dev-signed app once (your test tool usually does this), then check again.
	•	If it’s an MDM/corporate device, ask admin to allow Developer Mode.

D) Fixing iOS WebDriver (WebDriverAgent) issues

Use this if automation won’t start or you see WebDriver/WebDriverAgent errors.

Remove old WebDriverAgent from iPhone
	•	On iPhone, delete WebDriverAgent or WebDriverAgentRunner like a normal app
(or Settings > General > iPhone Storage → find app → Delete App).

Remove from Xcode (Installed Apps)
	1.	On Mac, open Xcode > Window > Devices and Simulators.
	2.	Select the iPhone.
	3.	In Installed Apps, select WebDriverAgentRunner → press Delete (or right-click Uninstall).

Re-install WebDriverAgent
	•	Option A (recommended): Start your automation (e.g., Appium). It will build & install WDA automatically.
	•	Option B (manual): Open the WebDriverAgent project in Xcode → target WebDriverAgentRunner → set Signing Team → choose the device as Destination → press Run.

On iPhone, if prompted, Trust Developer (Settings > General > VPN & Device Management). Accept any permission pop-ups.

If problems continue
	•	In Xcode: Product > Clean Build Folder (Shift+Cmd+K).
	•	Delete WDA Derived Data: Xcode > Settings > Locations → open Derived Data folder → delete WebDriverAgent.
	•	Restart the iPhone and try again.

E) Restarting the Mac
	1.	Click Apple menu () > Restart.
	2.	If the Mac is frozen: hold the Touch ID / Power button ~10 seconds to force shutdown, then press again to turn on.
	3.	If devices don’t show up after reboot, unplug/re-plug cables or hubs.

F) If Developer Mode is NOT ON (quick fixes)

iPhone
	•	iOS 16+, cable connected, Xcode open, device Trusted.
	•	Toggle Settings > Privacy & Security > Developer Mode.
	•	If still missing, install/run a dev-signed app once; check again.
	•	MDM/Company phones may require admin to enable.

Android
	•	Ensure Developer options = On and USB debugging = On (see Section B).
	•	When connecting, pick USB mode File Transfer if prompted.
	•	If not detected: try another cable/port; toggle USB debugging Off/On; reconnect and Always allow.

G) Quick pre-test checklist

☐ Phones restarted today
☐ Android: Developer options + USB debugging ON
☐ iPhone: Developer Mode ON + device Trusted with the Mac
☐ If iOS failed before: WebDriverAgent re-installed
☐ Cables firmly connected (try a different cable if a device is missing)

